    "lean": "```lean\n/-\n  Warehouse History Language (WHL) — a Lean-core-compatible formal language for\n  describing a replicated warehouse reservation snapshot as a bag of records.\n\n  This file declares a language, not a translation of any particular corpus.\n  It contains no quotations and no per-fragment translations.\n\n  The language is designed to be usable with Lean core alone (no Mathlib).\n  It uses only `Nat`, `List`, `Option`, `Bool`, and simple inductive types.\n  A `Bag` is represented as a `List`; bag equality is not declared, because\n  the language is about records and their relations, not about quotienting.\n\n  Primitive sorts:\n    - `Booking`  : opaque identifiers for bookings.\n    - `Revision` : natural numbers; higher is greater authority.\n    - `Quantity` : natural numbers. (Negative availability is handled separately.)\n    - `Time`     : natural numbers for logical time and expiry.\n\n  Record kinds:\n    - `hold`    : a hold of some quantity with an expiry.\n    - `release` : a release; quantity and expiry are still carried, because the\n                  language does not force a single meaning for them.\n\n  A `Record` is a quintuple (booking, revision, kind, quantity, expiry).\n  A `Snapshot` is a capacity, a logical time, and a list of records.\n\n  The language provides relations for:\n    - exact duplication (all five fields equal)\n    - revision dominance within a booking\n    - greatest revision within a booking\n    - active vs expired holds relative to logical time\n    - reservation effect of a record (zero for releases and expired holds;\n      quantity for active holds)\n    - alternative records at a greatest revision\n    - per-booking possible reservations (as sets of quantities)\n    - global availability bounds (as intervals over integers)\n\n  Conjectures, commitments and refutations are expressed as Lean `Prop`s.\n  The language does not assert which conjectures are true; it provides the\n  vocabulary to state them and to record refutations as proofs of negation\n  or as explicit counterexamples.\n\n  This construction permits: precise statements about which records remain\n  live under a given reading of supersession; separation of structural\n  supersession from evaluative expiry; explicit representation of unresolved\n  alternatives as sets of quantities; and formal criticism of rules by\n  stating alternative rules and deriving different results.\n\n  It may obstruct: reasoning about replication as such, because the snapshot\n  is a single list and the language has no primitive for multiple replicas;\n  reasoning about physical resource sharing beyond capacity, because capacity\n  is the only global constraint; and reasoning about the provenance or\n  authority of deliveries, because delivery is not modelled.\n-/\n\n/-! ## Primitive sorts -/\n\naxiom Booking : Type\naxiom Revision : Type\naxiom Quantity : Type\naxiom Time : Type\n\naxiom revision_le : Revision → Revision → Prop\naxiom revision_lt : Revision → Revision → Prop\naxiom revision_le_refl : ∀ r, revision_le r r\naxiom revision_lt_iff : ∀ r s, revision_lt r s ↔ revision_le r s ∧ ¬ revision_le s r\n\n/-! ## Record kinds and records -/\n\ninductive Kind : Type\n| hold : Kind\n| release : Kind\n\nstructure Record : Type := \n  (booking : Booking)\n  (revision : Revision)\n  (kind : Kind)\n  (quantity : Quantity)\n  (expiry : Time)\n\n/-! ## Snapshot -/\n\nstructure Snapshot : Type :=\n  (capacity : Quantity)\n  (logical_time : Time)\n  (records : List Record)\n\n/-! ## Basic relations on records -/\n\ndef exact_duplicate (r s : Record) : Prop :=\n  r.booking = s.booking ∧\n  r.revision = s.revision ∧\n  r.kind = s.kind ∧\n  r.quantity = s.quantity ∧\n  r.expiry = s.expiry\n\n/-- `s` has greater revision than `r` for the same booking. -/\ndef higher_revision (r s : Record) : Prop :=\n  r.booking = s.booking ∧ revision_lt r.revision s.revision\n\n/-- `r` is dominated within its booking by some record in the list. -/\ndef dominated_in (rs : List Record) (r : Record) : Prop :=\n  ∃ s, s ∈ rs ∧ higher_revision r s\n\n/-- `r` has greatest revision among records of its booking in `rs`. -/\ndef greatest_revision_in (rs : List Record) (r : Record) : Prop :=\n  r ∈ rs ∧ ¬ dominated_in rs r\n\n/-- Active hold relative to logical time: kind is hold and logical time has not\n    passed expiry. -/\ndef active_hold (t : Time) (r : Record) : Prop :=\n  r.kind = Kind.hold ∧ ¬ (t > r.expiry)\n\n/-- Expired hold relative to logical time. -/\ndef expired_hold (t : Time) (r : Record) : Prop :=\n  r.kind = Kind.hold ∧ t > r.expiry\n\n/-- Reservation effect under the \"structural supersession, evaluative expiry\" reading. -/\ndef reserves_effect (t : Time) (r : Record) : Quantity :=\n  match r.kind with\n  | Kind.hold => if t > r.expiry then 0 else r.quantity\n  | Kind.release => 0\n\n/-! ## Alternatives at greatest revision -/\n\n/-- Two records are alternatives at a greatest revision for a booking if they\n    are both in the snapshot, both have the greatest revision for that booking,\n    and they are not exact duplicates. -/\ndef alternatives_at_greatest (rs : List Record) (r s : Record) : Prop :=\n  r.booking = s.booking ∧\n  greatest_revision_in rs r ∧\n  greatest_revision_in rs s ∧\n  ¬ exact_duplicate r s\n\n/-- The set of quantities that a booking could reserve under the unresolved-alternatives\n    reading, given a snapshot. Represented as a list of quantities (may contain duplicates).\n    For each record at the greatest revision for the booking, include its reservation effect.\n    If no such record exists, the list is empty (booking reserves nothing). -/\ndef possible_reservations (snap : Snapshot) (b : Booking) : List Quantity :=\n  let rs := snap.records;\n  let t := snap.logical_time;\n  (rs.filter (λ r, r.booking = b ∧ greatest_revision_in rs r)).map (reserves_effect t)\n\n/-! ## Availability bounds -/\n\n/-- Unary minus for natural numbers encoded as integers, to allow negative availability. -/\ninductive Int : Type\n| of_nat : Nat → Int\n| neg_succ_of_nat : Nat → Int\n\nnamespace Int\ndef sub_nat (a b : Nat) : Int :=\n  if a ≥ b then Int.of_nat (a - b) else Int.neg_succ_of_nat (b - a - 1)\nend Int\n\n/-- Minimum quantity in a list, if any. -/\ndef list_min? : List Quantity → Option Quantity\n| [] => none\n| (x :: xs) => some (xs.foldl (λ acc y, if y < acc then y else acc) x)\n\n/-- Maximum quantity in a list, if any. -/\ndef list_max? : List Quantity → Option Quantity\n| [] => none\n| (x :: xs) => some (xs.foldl (λ acc y, if y > acc then y else acc) x)\n\n/-- Per-booking lower bound: minimum possible reservation, or 0 if none. -/\ndef booking_lower_bound (snap : Snapshot) (b : Booking) : Quantity :=\n  match list_min? (possible_reservations snap b) with\n  | none => 0\n  | some q => q\n\n/-- Per-booking upper bound: maximum possible reservation, or 0 if none. -/\ndef booking_upper_bound (snap : Snapshot) (b : Booking) : Quantity :=\n  match list_max? (possible_reservations snap b) with\n  | none => 0\n  | some q => q\n\n/-- Global lower availability bound: capacity minus sum of per-booking lower bounds. -/\ndef availability_lower_bound (snap : Snapshot) (bookings : List Booking) : Int :=\n  let total_lower := bookings.foldl (λ acc b, acc + booking_lower_bound snap b) 0;\n  Int.sub_nat snap.capacity total_lower\n\n/-- Global upper availability bound: capacity minus sum of per-booking upper bounds. -/\ndef availability_upper_bound (snap : Snapshot) (bookings : List Booking) : Int :=\n  let total_upper := bookings.foldl (λ acc b, acc + booking_upper_bound snap b) 0;\n  Int.sub_nat snap.capacity total_upper\n\n/-! ## Conjectures, commitments, refutations -/\n\n/-- A conjecture is a proposition about a snapshot. -/\ndef Conjecture : Type := Snapshot → Prop\n\n/-- A commitment is a proposition that a reasoner endorses. -/\ndef Commitment : Type := Prop\n\n/-- A refutation of a conjecture is a snapshot together with a proof that the\n    conjecture does not hold for that snapshot. -/\nstructure Refutation (c : Conjecture) : Type :=\n  (snap : Snapshot)\n  (not_c : ¬ c snap)\n\n/-- Example schema: a rule states a reservation function for a single record. -/\ndef ReservationRule : Type := Time → Record → Quantity\n\n/-- Two rules disagree on a snapshot if they assign different possible reservations\n    to some booking. -/\ndef rules_disagree (r1 r2 : ReservationRule) (snap : Snapshot) : Prop :=\n  ∃ b, possible_reservations_with r1 snap b ≠ possible_reservations_with r2 snap b\n\n/-- Reservation possibilities parameterized by a rule. -/\ndef possible_reservations_with (rule : ReservationRule) (snap : Snapshot) (b : Booking) : List Quantity :=\n  let rs := snap.records;\n  let t := snap.logical_time;\n  (rs.filter (λ r, r.booking = b ∧ greatest_revision_in rs r)).map (rule t)\n\n/-- Alternative rule: expiry deletes the record, so a lower revision can become greatest. -/\ndef expired_deletes_rule (t : Time) (r : Record) : Quantity :=\n  match r.kind with\n  | Kind.hold => if t > r.expiry then 0 else r.quantity\n  | Kind.release => 0\n\n/-- The standard rule and the deletion rule can differ in which records are\n    considered greatest. This is stated as a possible disagreement, not resolved. -/\ndef deletion_changes_greatest (snap : Snapshot) : Prop :=\n  ∃ b r, greatest_revision_in snap.records r ∧\n         r.booking = b ∧\n         expired_hold snap.logical_time r ∧\n         ∃ s, s ∈ snap.records ∧ s.booking = b ∧\n              revision_lt s.revision r.revision ∧\n              active_hold snap.logical_time s\n\n/-! ## Notes on intended meaning -/\n\n/- \n  The language treats the snapshot as a bag of records with no order.\n  Order is not primitive; membership and filtering are the only structural\n  operations.\n\n  \"Duplicate deliveries convey no additional authority\" is represented by\n  `exact_duplicate`: two records with all five fields equal are inert with\n  respect to each other. The language does not say that near-duplicates are\n  inert; they may be alternatives.\n\n  \"A higher revision supersedes lower revisions\" is represented by\n  `greater_revision_in`, which ignores lower revisions for the same booking.\n  It does not cross booking boundaries, because `higher_revision` requires\n  equality of booking.\n\n  \"A release or expired hold can reserve zero while an active hold reserves its\n  quantity\" is represented by `reserves_effect`. This is one reading; the\n  alternative reading where expiry deletes the record is represented by\n  `expired_deletes_rule`, but the language does not choose between them.\n\n  \"Availability can be negative\" is represented by `Int.sub_nat`, which yields\n  an integer that can be negative.\n\n  \"Unresolved alternatives\" is represented by `possible_reservations`, which\n  is a list of quantities, not a single quantity. Different bookings have\n  separate lists; there is no global choice function.\n\n  \"Different bookings need not share the same choice\" is represented by the\n  absence of any mechanism that forces a single choice across bookings.\n\n  \"What a criticism targets\" is represented by the separation between\n  conjectures about rules (`ReservationRule`) and conjectures about data\n  (`Record` and `Snapshot`). A refutation targets a conjecture by exhibiting\n  a snapshot for which it fails.\n\n  \"Source assumptions\" are not primitive. The language can state them as\n  conjectures or commitments, but it does not encode a notion of assumption\n  dependence. This is an expressive limitation.\n\n  \"Replication\" is not primitive. The snapshot is a single list. The language\n  cannot distinguish union, intersection, or replica-relative views. This is\n  an expressive limitation.\n\n  \"Physical resource sharing\" is not primitive beyond capacity. The language\n  cannot express incompatibility between alternatives of different bookings\n  except through the capacity sum. This is an expressive limitation.\n\n  \"Logical time per replica\" is not primitive. There is one logical time in\n  the snapshot. The language can state conjectures about replica-relative\n  time but cannot model multiple times directly. This is an expressive\n  limitation.\n\n  The language does not assert that any conjecture is true. It provides\n  vocabulary for stating conjectures, commitments, and refutations.\n-/\n```",
    "nonlean": "Non-Lean Semantic Class: **Rival-Story Semantics (RSS)**\n\nRSS is a non-Lean, noncomputable semantic class for the frozen warehouse prose. It is not a proof assistant language. It is a way of assigning meanings to statements about a replicated warehouse snapshot by treating the snapshot as a collection of rival stories rather than as a database. It is deliberately noncomputable: it does not provide an algorithm for resolving conflicts, and it treats any demand for a single answer as a projection that must be justified outside the semantics.\n\n**Primitives**\n\n1. **Story**: a story is a claim about what is promised for a booking. A story is not a record. Records are evidence for stories. A story has a booking, a revision, a kind (hold or release), a quantity, an expiry, and a provenance (which delivery or set of deliveries it is drawn from). Provenance is not ordered; it is a set of delivery events.\n\n2. **Bag**: an unordered collection of deliveries. A delivery is an event that makes a record available. Deliveries are not ordered, may be repeated, and may differ in logical time if they come from different replicas. The bag is the raw material, not the story.\n\n3. **Coordinate**: a capacity and a logical time. A coordinate can be shared or replica-relative. RSS treats coordinates as parameters of a viewpoint, not as absolute facts.\n\n4. **Viewpoint**: a pair (coordinate, bag). A viewpoint is what a reasoner sees. Different viewpoints may disagree about logical time, capacity, and which deliveries are present.\n\n5. **Conflict**: a situation in which two or more stories for the same booking at the same greatest revision are not exact duplicates. Conflict is not error; it is material.\n\n6. **Liveness**: a story is live under a viewpoint if it is not superseded by a higher revision for the same booking within that viewpoint, and if its kind and expiry are considered under the viewpoint's reading of expiry.\n\n7. **Reading**: a reading is a policy that decides how to treat expiry, releases, duplicates, and ties. Readings are not part of the snapshot; they are supplied by reasoners. RSS does not choose a reading. It compares readings.\n\n8. **Projection**: a function from a viewpoint and a reading to a single answer (for example, a single availability number). Projections are allowed but not privileged. A projection is always a loss of information.\n\n**Relations**\n\n- **Exact duplication**: two deliveries are exact duplicates if all record fields and provenance are equal. Exact duplicates add no authority.\n- **Near-duplication**: two deliveries share booking and revision but differ in kind, quantity, or expiry. Near-duplicates are candidates for conflict. RSS does not decide whether they are duplicates or alternatives; it records the disagreement.\n- **Supersession**: for a given booking, a story with higher revision supersedes a story with lower revision. Supersession is structural within a reading; whether it is structural across readings is a matter of conjecture.\n- **Expiry effect**: under one reading, an expired hold reserves zero but remains superseding; under another, an expired hold is deleted and lower revisions can become live. RSS represents both as readings, not as facts.\n- **Release effect**: under one reading, a release at a lower revision cannot cancel a hold at a higher revision; under another, cancellation is possible. RSS represents both as readings.\n- **Alternative compatibility**: two stories from different bookings may be compatible or incompatible depending on unstated physical resources. RSS marks this as invisible unless a viewpoint includes a resource model.\n- **Negative availability**: availability is a derived quantity that can be negative under a reading. RSS treats the sign as a signal, not an error, and treats the set of reservations as more informative than the sign.\n\n**Combinations**\n\nRSS combines primitives into **accounts**. An account is a viewpoint, a reading, and